# Case Analysis: W v Registrar of Marriages [2013] HKCFA 39

## 1. Summary of Facts

W is a Hong Kong permanent resident in her thirties who was registered as male at birth. From an early age, she perceived herself as female and was diagnosed with gender identity disorder (transsexualism). She underwent the full course of accepted medical treatment: psychiatric assessment, hormonal treatment, a supervised two-year "real life experience" living as a woman, an orchidectomy performed in Thailand in 2007, and full sex reassignment surgery (SRS) in 2008 at Hong Kong Hospital Authority facilities, involving removal of her penis and construction of an artificial vagina enabling sexual intercourse with a man.

Following the surgery, the Hospital Authority issued a certificate stating her gender had changed to female. She was issued a new Hong Kong identity card and passport recording her sex as female, and she lives in all respects as a woman. However, she retains male XY chromosomes and cannot bear children.

W wished to marry her male partner. The Registrar of Marriages refused to permit the marriage, relying on the legal position (derived from the English case of *Corbett v Corbett*) that a person's biological sex is fixed at birth and cannot be altered by surgery, so that for marriage purposes W remained male. W brought judicial review proceedings challenging this decision. She lost at first instance and in the Court of Appeal, and appealed to the Court of Final Appeal.

## 2. Summary of the Parties' Arguments

**The Appellant (W):** Lord Pannick QC argued that W should be recognized as a woman for the purposes of marriage. It was expressly *not* argued that same-sex marriage should be permitted; rather, the contention was that W *is* a woman and therefore entitled to marry a person of the opposite sex. Two questions were certified: (1) whether, on the true construction of the Marriage Ordinance, "woman" and "female" include a post-operative male-to-female transsexual; and (2) if not, whether those provisions are unconstitutional as violating W's right to marry under Article 37 of the Basic Law and Article 19(2) of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights (and/or her privacy rights under Article 14). The appellant relied on the ECtHR's decision in *Goodwin v UK*, the international trend toward recognition of acquired gender, medical advances in treating transsexualism, and the argument that the constitutional provisions are "living instruments" to be interpreted in light of changing circumstances.

**The Respondent (Registrar of Marriages):** Ms Carss-Frisk QC argued that the words "man," "woman," "male," and "female" in the Marriage Ordinance and Matrimonial Causes Ordinance refer to biological sex fixed at birth, in accordance with the *Corbett* criteria (chromosomal, gonadal, and genital factors). She submitted that when Hong Kong enacted section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance in 1972, reproducing the UK's Nullity of Marriage Act 1971, the legislature deliberately endorsed *Corbett*. On the constitutional question, she argued that the framers of the Basic Law and Bill of Rights must have intended the traditional, biological concept of marriage; that there was no evidence of social consensus in Hong Kong or international consensus under the ICCPR supporting transsexual marriage; that procreation remains an important feature of marriage (supported by the non-consummation ground); and that in any event, the far-reaching ramifications of recognizing acquired gender for marriage were so complex that the Court should leave the matter to the legislature rather than intervene by remedial interpretation.

## 3. The Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

The Court of Final Appeal, by a 4–1 majority (Chan PJ dissenting; Lord Hoffmann NPJ agreeing with the joint judgment), allowed the appeal. The judgment of Ma CJ and Ribeiro PJ contains the following principles:

**(a) Statutory construction: the *Corbett* criteria were deliberately imported into Hong Kong law.** The Court held that, as a matter of pure statutory construction, the Registrar had *not* misconstrued the Ordinances. Section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance was enacted in 1972 in materially identical terms to section 1(c) of the English Nullity of Marriage Act 1971, which was itself passed shortly after *Corbett v Corbett* to put Ormrod J's decision on a statutory footing. The Hong Kong legislature therefore "consciously and expressly adopted" (or, more precisely, implicitly adopted) the *Corbett* approach: that sex for marriage purposes is determined by chromosomal, gonadal, and genital criteria fixed at birth, ignoring any operative intervention. The Court also clarified that "ordinary meaning" arguments are unhelpful; context and purpose are crucial, and a person may be a "woman" for some legal purposes (identity cards, passports, prisons, toilets) but not others. The Court rejected the argument that non-consummation provisions demonstrate that procreation is essential to marriage, noting that consummation requires only coital penetration, not conception, and that consummation is legally possible with a surgically constructed vagina.

**(b) The constitutional right to marry is a "strong right" whose essence cannot be impaired.** Article 37 of the Basic Law and Article 19(2) of the Bill of Rights guarantee the right to marry. While the right is necessarily subject to legal regulation (monogamy, marriageable age, prohibited degrees of consanguinity), such regulation "must not operate so as to impair the very essence of that right." Drawing on *R (Baiai)*, the Court characterized the right to marry as a "strong right"—notably, unlike qualified Convention rights, it contains no express limitation clause.

**(c) The *Corbett* criteria are constitutionally deficient because they are incomplete.** The Court held that confining the assessment of sexual identity to biological criteria fixed at birth is "too restrictive" and "a blinkered view." The proper approach is to consider *all* circumstances—biological, psychological, and social—relevant to assessing an individual's sexual identity **at the time of the (proposed) marriage**. This is a central and novel holding: the Court rejected the premise underlying *Corbett*—that procreative heterosexual intercourse is the essential constituent of marriage—observing that procreation has never been a legal condition of marriage (the infertile, the elderly, and the contracepting may marry) and that the institution of marriage has evolved dramatically in contemporary multi-cultural Hong Kong. The Court also emphasized the fundamental importance of the psychological dimension of gender identity, criticizing Ormrod J's characterization of April Ashley as a "pastiche of femininity" resembling a "female impersonator" as betraying a failure to recognize the potency of psychological compulsion. It agreed with the ECtHR in *Goodwin* that the chromosomal element need not take on decisive significance.

**(d) Denying transsexuals the right to marry in their acquired gender impairs the very essence of the right.** Because irreversible surgery and implacable psychological identity mean a post-operative transsexual woman cannot meaningfully marry as a man, the statutory provisions, as construed, prevented W from marrying *at all*. Following *Goodwin*, the Court held this was an unconstitutional impairment of the essence of the right to marry under Article 37 and Article 19(2). (The privacy argument under Article 14 was therefore not addressed.)

**(e) Rejection of the "consensus" argument.** The Court refused to require evidence of a social consensus in Hong Kong (or international consensus) before recognizing minority rights. It held that the ECtHR's margin-of-appreciation/consensus practice has no bearing on the HKSAR court's role in interpreting its own constitution, and—more fundamentally—that "reliance on the absence of a majority consensus as a reason for rejecting a minority's claim is inimical in principle to fundamental rights." This is a significant and controversial principle: constitutional rights protect minorities precisely against majority preferences.

**(f) The "living instrument" doctrine applied.** The Basic Law and Bill of Rights are living instruments intended to meet changing needs and circumstances. Changed medical understanding of transsexualism, official Hong Kong practice (publicly funded SRS, gender-change certificates, new identity cards and passports), and evolving social attitudes compelled a departure from the position that prevailed when the constitutional provisions were framed.

**(g) Remedial interpretation rather than striking down; suspension of the order.** The Court held it had a *duty* (not a discretion) to act on unconstitutionality, either by striking down or by remedial interpretation (reading down, reading in, severance), subject to the limit that it cannot adopt a cure "so fundamentally at odds with the intent of the legislation" that legislative deliberation is required. Here, a remedial interpretation was appropriate: sections 40 MO and 20(1)(d) MCO must be read so that "woman"/"female" includes a post-operative male-to-female transsexual person whose gender has been certified by an appropriate medical authority as changed by SRS. The Court deliberately declined to lay down a universal bright-line test for less extensive cases, leaving open whether persons who have undergone less than full SRS qualify. The operation of the declarations was suspended for 12 months to allow the legislature to consider comprehensive legislation (the Court commended the UK Gender Recognition Act 2004 as a model, including an expert-panel certification system, and identified areas needing attention: effects on existing marriages, parenting, pensions, discrimination, succession, sport, gender-specific offences, and disclosure of gender history). If no legislation is enacted, W's entitlement takes effect regardless.

**(h) Bokhary NPJ's concurring approach.** Bokhary NPJ took a somewhat different route: he held that anyone certified by an appropriate medical authority as having had their sex reassigned by surgery *is*, in the eyes of Hong Kong law, a person of the reassigned sex, and that the constitutional right to marry necessarily extends to marriage in the reassigned capacity—"to say that it has no application to post-operative transsexuals is to deny their humanity." He also observed that a remedial interpretation can be a *less* radical course than a striking-down order, and contrasted the UK's weak declaration of incompatibility with Hong Kong's entrenched constitution.

**(i) Chan PJ's dissent.** Chan PJ dissented, holding that "man" and "woman" in Article 37 bear their ordinary biological meaning; that there was insufficient evidence of changed social attitudes in Hong Kong to justify an "updated interpretation"; that the Court's role is to give effect to changed social policy, not to introduce new social policy, which belongs to the democratic process; and that the far-reaching ramifications required comprehensive legislative review rather than judicial intervention—though he acknowledged "there is no logical reason why full recognition should not be extended" to transsexuals and called for reform "as soon as practicable."

## 4. Something Striking or Remarkable

Several features of this case are extraordinary:

**The human tragedy underlying the litigation.** The judgment records the suicides of two Hong Kong transgender women—Louise Chan, who was stalked and "outed" by the media in 2003, losing her job, and who killed herself in 2004, followed two days later by Sasha Moon. The case is thus not an abstract rights dispute but sits against a backdrop of documented, fatal consequences of non-recognition.

**The state's internal incoherence, exposed by the litigation.** The Hong Kong Government had *funded* W's sex reassignment surgery, certified her gender as changed, issued her a female identity card and passport, and would have imprisoned her in a women's prison—yet refused to let her marry a man, insisting she was legally male. The Court noted this left post-operative transsexuals in an "intermediate zone, not quite one gender or the other." Bokhary NPJ pointed out the logical absurdity bluntly: "There is no logical reason why full recognition should not be extended to enable them to marry in their reassigned gender."

**The judicial dismantling of *Corbett*'s rhetoric.** The Court's frank criticism of the 1970 judgment is remarkable: it condemned Ormrod J's description of April Ashley as resembling "the accomplished female impersonator" with a convincing "pastiche of femininity" as language suggesting artifice and betraying a failure to grasp the psychological reality of transsexualism. A final appellate court thus openly repudiated not just the holding but the tone and premises of a foundational precedent.

**The constitutional irony of the dissent.** Chan PJ, while dismissing the appeal, conceded that the Government's position was illogical and urged legislative reform "as soon as practicable"—dissenting on institutional grounds while agreeing on the merits. Meanwhile, the majority's rejection of the "consensus" argument—invoking the Irish Chief Justice's question of how the will of the majority can dictate a court interpreting universal human rights—stands as a bold statement of counter-majoritarian judicial review in the Hong Kong context.

**The Mainland comparison.** Bokhary NPJ noted that mainland China already recognized the right of post-operative transsexuals to marry in their acquired gender—meaning that upholding W's right would keep Hong Kong "fully within the international trend," a striking inversion of the usual assumption that Hong Kong is more liberal than the Mainland on such matters. Indeed, the Court catalogued that Hong Kong was out of step with the Mainland, Singapore, Japan, South Korea, India, Indonesia, Australia, New Zealand, Canada, and all 47 Council of Europe states.

**The procedural innovation.** The Court suspended its declarations for twelve months—balancing constitutional vindication with legislative breathing space—and helpfully mapped out the entire legislative landscape (using the UK Gender Recognition Act 2004 as a template) while making clear that W's rights would take effect even if the legislature did nothing. This "dialogic" remedy, combining immediate constitutional principle with deferred effect, is a notable model of constitutional adjudication. [end of text]


